A one-way push on your own initiative, no reply expected. Only server → client and microservice → parent server; the reverse directions go through REQ. An announcement that arrived for you → `sdh-cross-repo-announce-read`.

## 0. Is it an announcement?

| Situation | Channel |
|---|---|
| The recipient asked for X and you did it | REQ receipt (`sdh-cross-repo-req-answer`) |
| Your own change affects the recipient | announcement |
| Work for recipient A has a side effect on B | announcement to B |
| An internal rework forces the recipient's integration to adjust | announcement |
| The recipient's REQ was closed (✓) before you shipped it | announcement — it is now the release receipt (threshold below does not apply) |

Threshold: write one only if the recipient must change code, config or behaviour, or acknowledge a deprecation. A new endpoint they may adopt later reaches them through the contract sync.

## 1. Recipients — from your `docs/project.md`

| Direction | Rows | You write |
|---|---|---|
| Server → client | `## Repositories`, role `client` / `admin_client` / `test_client` | `docs/InOutBox/outbox/<client-repo>/ANNOUNCE-NNN_<slug>.md` — one file per recipient |
| Microservice → parent | `## Parent projects`, row of the target server | `docs/InOutBox/outbox/<parent-repo>/ANNOUNCE-NNN_<slug>.md` |

No path (`_no local path configured_`) or `⚠ server repo not resolvable` → do not write; tell the user or file a `T-` task. Folder names use the last segment of the repo name; take yours from the `## Repositories` row whose path is the current directory.

## 2. Number — from `docs/InOutBox/registry/<peer>.md`

- `max` over every `registry/*.md` line plus the frozen legacy ledgers + 1 — the same global counter every other kind uses, incoming included. Write the `out` line into `registry/<recipient>.md` in the same pass; the app never writes the registry.
- Ledger missing → seed it from the pair's `ANNOUNCE-*.md` (and any frozen `docs/*-announcements/`) first; never restart at `001` while history exists.
- In a shared repo: sync first, then write the ledger line and the body — `max+1` without a current `dev` collides.

## 3. File — in your own `outbox/`

```markdown
# ANNOUNCE-NNN: <headline>

## Why this matters to you
<impact on the recipient>

## What changed
<the change on your side>

## What you need to do
<steps: integration / migration / acknowledgement>

## References
- <commit / endpoint>
```

Your copy in `outbox/` is the master: the next sync mirrors the body into each recipient's `docs/InOutBox/inbox/<you>/` (and onto the shared `dev` branch for a shared repo). Recipients ack with an empty `.ack/<key>` marker; the app transports the markers back and deletes body and markers on both sides once every expected key has arrived. Your later edits to the body reset that ack group — ship the final text.

The old carve-out is gone: you never write into another repo — `outbox/` is the only place you touch, same as REQ and peer exchanges.
